\mytoc{Introduction}{Chapter 1: Introduction}{Chapter 2: Basics of LaTeX}{Chapter 3: Advanced TeX Features}

\end{document}
```

In this example, we first define the `mytoc` command which takes one optional argument (`#1`) that specifies the number of sections in the TOC. We then use the `\tableofcontents` command from the `toc` package to create the actual TOC structure.

To include the TOC in our document, we simply call the `mytoc` command and pass it the desired section numbers as arguments. In this case, we have defined three sections: "Introduction", "Basics of LaTeX", and "Advanced TeX Features". The output will look something like this:

```
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{toc}

\mytoc{Introduction}{Chapter 1: Introduction}{Chapter 2: Basics of LaTeX}{Chapter 3: Advanced TeX Features}

\end{document}
```

